Subject: [SE4-P6] mf: never hold the audio renderer lock across audio driver calls

The audio renderer held its lock, renderer->cs, across calls into the audio
driver: IAudioClient::Start, Stop and Reset when the presentation clock
started, paused, stopped or restarted it and when the stream was flushed,
IAudioClient::Initialize when the media type was set, and Stop, Reset and
Release when the client was torn down. The presentation clock reads the
audio position through the same lock on every video frame, so a per-frame
query such as IMFMediaEngine::OnVideoStreamTick or GetCurrentTime waited
behind a driver call. A driver call waits for the audio server, and when the
server does not answer, for as long as it does not: the thread that
displays the video stopped for the whole time, although the video itself
was running.

No thread holds renderer->cs across a call that changes the state of the
audio client, or that creates, initialises or destroys it, any more. Such
calls are made on a reference to the client with the lock dropped, and the
renderer state is revalidated afterwards against a generation counter, which
changes whenever the client is replaced or released; results for a client
that is gone are dropped. The calls on the volume interfaces are made the
same way. A shutdown during a driver call therefore never waits for it: it
detaches the client and hands it, with the event the client signals, to a
worker thread of the default thread pool, which stops and releases it and
then closes the event, and the thread that is in the call holds its own
reference.

Clock state transitions (start, stop, pause, restart, flush) and the
replacement of the audio client are serialised by a new lock, transition_cs,
which is taken before renderer->cs and never by the clock read path. A
transition sets the state it is going to before it drops renderer->cs. While
a transition is in flight, clock reads return the time it freezes: the time
reached so far for a pause or a flush, 0 for a stop. A start leaves the
position unchanged until the client runs. The render callback does not touch
the client while a transition is driving it, or while there is none, and
keeps its wait armed and the queued samples. Reads never wait for the
transition, and the clock states, the stream sink events, their order and
the results reported to the callers are the same as before.

The audio position and the buffer calls made by the render callback are
quick queries that do not wait for the server; they stay under the lock.

A driver call that takes 100 ms or more is reported with a warning, and one
that takes a second or more with an error, naming the call, its duration,
the result and the thread, at most once per second per renderer and
severity, so that a stalled audio server shows in the log.

While a start is blocked in the driver, the clock does not advance, so the
video waits for the audio, as it did, but the thread that displays it is
free.

