+/* The renderer lock, renderer->cs, protects the renderer's own state only. The presentation clock reads the audio
+ * position under it on every video frame, and a call into the audio driver can wait for an unresponsive audio
+ * server, so no thread may hold renderer->cs across such a call.
+ *
+ * Calls that change the state of the audio client, or replace the client, are transitions. A transition takes
+ * renderer->transition_cs, then renderer->cs, and drops renderer->cs for its driver calls, which it makes on a
+ * reference to the client. The clock read path never takes transition_cs. Lock order is transition_cs, then cs.
+ *
+ * A transition sets the state it is going to, and SAR_CLIENT_BUSY, under renderer->cs before it drops it, and
+ * revalidates against client_generation after taking it again; the generation changes whenever the client is
+ * replaced or released, and results for a client that is gone are dropped. The render path uses the client only
+ * when no transition is driving it: while SAR_CLIENT_BUSY is set, or while there is no client, it leaves the
+ * client and the queued samples alone.
+ *
+ * A transition that moves the position (pause, stop, flush) freezes the time the clock read path returns for its
+ * duration: the time reached so far, or 0 for a stop. A start leaves the position unchanged until the client runs.
+ *
+ * The resources the client uses go with the client: a shutdown hands the client, and the event it signals, to a
+ * worker thread of the default thread pool, which pins this module. A worker stalled in the driver occupies one
+ * thread of that pool until the driver call returns.
+ *
+ * The rate limit on the diagnostics is not locked: a call that is logged twice in a second is harmless. A call that
+ * never returns logs nothing; the next transition waiting for transition_cs for it is reported by the lock wait of
+ * the system. */
